\title{crywolf: Bayesian false-positive occupancy models with calibration checks}
\description{
Automated detectors, such as acoustic classifiers, return false positives,
and occupancy models that ignore them overestimate occupancy and distort
covariate effects. crywolf fits the multiple-methods (site-confirmation)
false-positive occupancy model of Miller et al. (2011), in which a subset of
detections is verified and therefore certain, and asks whether the
correction is calibrated for a given study design.

The package has three parts:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Data and model.} \code{\link[=fp_data]{fp_data()}} (or \code{\link[=fp_data_wide]{fp_data_wide()}}) assembles the
detections, and \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}} fits the model in Stan through 'cmdstanr'.
The false-positive rate can be constant (\code{fp = ~ 1}) or depend on site
covariates (\code{fp = ~ w}, site- or occasion-level), with an optional site
random effect (\code{random_fp = TRUE}); the verified method can have
covariates (\code{ver}), runs of detections can be modelled (\code{markov}), and
the true-detection process accepts
observation covariates, a recorder random effect, and a site random
effect (\code{random_det = TRUE}). The
observation-confirmation design of Chambert et al. (2015), in which an
expert checks the detections of some automated occasions, is supported
through the \code{confirmed} argument of \code{\link[=fp_data]{fp_data()}}. \code{\link[=fp_read_birdnet]{fp_read_birdnet()}},
\code{\link[=fp_read_validation]{fp_read_validation()}}, and \code{\link[=fp_aggregate]{fp_aggregate()}} build the data from
classifier output, and \link{curiango} holds a real dataset.
\item \strong{Checking and plotting.} \code{\link[=fp_prior_predictive]{fp_prior_predictive()}} checks the priors
against the data, \code{\link[=fp_ppc]{fp_ppc()}} runs a posterior predictive check, and
\code{\link[=plot.fp_occu]{plot.fp_occu()}}, \code{\link[=fp_effects]{fp_effects()}}, and \code{\link[=fp_sites]{fp_sites()}} show the
coefficients, the effect of a covariate, and the probability that each
site is occupied.
\item \strong{Simulation.} \code{\link[=simulate_fp]{simulate_fp()}} generates data under homogeneous,
unstructured, and structured ("confusion") false-positive regimes.
\item \strong{Calibration.} \code{\link[=fp_calibrate]{fp_calibrate()}} and \code{\link[=fp_verification_curve]{fp_verification_curve()}} fit the
model to many simulated datasets and report the coverage of the 95\%
credible intervals and the bias of the quantities of interest, so that
users can see whether the correction holds for their design and how much
verification it needs.
}

See \link{crywolf-output} for the names and interpretation of model parameters
and result fields.
